\section{Relation to the steady state}{

In a steady state the recruitment is the one returned by
\code{\link[=getRequiredRDD]{getRequiredRDD()}}, so there
\deqn{R_0 = \frac{R_{di}}{R_{dd}},}{R_0 = R_di / R_dd,}
the ratio of \code{getRDI(params)} to \code{getRequiredRDD(params)}. With
density-independent reproduction (\code{R_max = Inf}) every steady state
therefore has \eqn{R_0 = 1}. With Beverton-Holt reproduction the
recruitment is reduced by the factor \eqn{1 - r}, where \eqn{r} is the
reproduction level (see \code{\link[=reproduction_level]{reproduction_level()}}), so a steady state has
\eqn{R_0 = 1 / (1 - r)}, not 1.

The ratio \code{getRDI(params) / getRequiredRDD(params)} is \eqn{R_0} only in a
steady state, and it is 0/0 for a species that is absent. \code{getR0()} needs
neither, because it holds the rates fixed and solves for the abundance per
recruit. Because \eqn{R_0} is proportional to the reproductive efficiency
\code{erepro}, dividing \code{erepro} by \eqn{R_0} makes the steady state for the
current rates a steady state of a model with density-independent
reproduction.
